 ("ChessValidator", "The chess dictionary validator", 3, "PracticePrograms", ("is_valid_chessboard(board)", "The validator checks that every key is a square from a1 to h8, every value is a colour letter and a piece letter, each side has exactly one king, at most eight pawns and at most sixteen pieces, and returns True or False.", ("(lambda valid, b: (valid(b), valid({**b, 'a1': 'wK'}), valid({**b, 'i9': 'wP'}), valid({**b, **{c + '2': 'wP' for c in 'abcdefgh'}, 'a3': 'wP'})))(lambda b: all(len(s) == 2 and s[0] in 'abcdefgh' and s[1] in '12345678' and len(p) == 2 and p[0] in 'wb' and p[1] in 'PNBRQK' for s, p in b.items()) and all(list(b.values()).count(c + 'K') == 1 and list(b.values()).count(c + 'P') <= 8 and sum(p[0] == c for p in b.values()) <= 16 for c in 'wb'), %s)" % _BOOK, "(True, False, False, False)")), [
  (W, "The validator is a function that takes a chessboard dictionary and returns True when the board could be a position in a real game and False when a bug has produced an improper one. The task asks for exactly one king of each colour, at most 16 pieces for each player, of which at most eight are pawns, and every piece on a valid square [SW]. The task's own text writes the squares as '1a' to '8h' and the piece names as 'wpawn' and similar, while the chapter's model uses 'a1' to 'h8' and 'wP'; the solution follows the chapter's model."),
  (Y, "The model of the chapter accepts anything, so a function must guard it. Validation is the usual companion of a data structure: the more freely the structure can be changed, the more a check is needed before the data is trusted. A bug in a move command, such as a lost piece or a second king, shows up as a False."),
  (E, "Validators are in forms that check an e-mail address, in programs that check a loaded file and in tests that check an invariant. The invariant of the board, the statement that is always true of a legal board, is the list of rules above."),
  (H, "The function loops over board.items(). For each pair it checks the shape of the key, a letter a to h and a digit 1 to 8, and of the value, a letter w or b and one of PNBRQK, returning False at the first failure. It counts the pieces in a dictionary with count[piece] = count.get(piece, 0) + 1 and then checks, for each colour, that the count of the king is exactly 1, that of the pawn is at most 8, and the total at most 16. The book's board gives True; a second white king, a pawn on i9 and nine white pawns each give False."),
  (K, "The checks are not complete: a pawn on the first rank or two bishops on squares of one colour are not detected, and the task says that the list is close enough for the exercise. A board that passes is not a position that can be reached in a game. Check for the shape of every key and value before counting, or the count may be of nonsense."),
 ]),
